Recent analyses of lepton flavor universality (LFU) violating $b 	o c 	au 
u$ decays have yielded outcomes with varying results, presenting possible signs of new physics. Within the context of the $U_1$ leptoquark model, which addresses these anomalies and involves an interaction between a long-lived $U_1$ boson and a tau lepton, we investigate the impact on the $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decay. By comparing our calculated branching ratio with available experimental data, we find that it fits within the established range, but exhibits sensitivity to the $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ LFU violating transition. Specifically, an enhancement in the $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decay, induced by the $U_1$ boson contributing to the $b 	o c$ transition, could be in the order of $10^{-3}$ to $10^{-2}$, largely depending on the mass difference between the $U_1$ boson and the tau lepton. Exploiting the $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decay at LHCb, with the upcoming Run 3, offers a complementary approach to test the $U_1$ leptoquark model through the $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ transition.

